// SEVENTEEN generated_conformance_floor ROWS LEFT THIS ROSTER BECAUSE THE FIX THIS BRANCH CARRIES
// MADE THEM PASS, WHICH IS THIS ROSTER'S OWN REMOVAL PATH RATHER THAN A ROSTER EDIT. They were
// among the identities the required floor reported as KNOWN-RED-RUNTIME-ERRORED -- enrolled as
// expected-red but throwing rather than failing, so producing no verdict at all -- and every one
// of them threw for the same reason this branch repairs: the reference-closure collector narrowed
// on binding metadata its own producer never populates, so a name that was genuinely declared
// resolved to nothing and the call threw `no such function`.
//
// Once the closure is derived from lexical binders instead, the calls resolve and the witnesses
// answer. Every one of them answered PASS, and the floor duly reported them STALE-QUARANTINE --
// which IS gating (claim_executor `required_floor_outcome_is_clean` lists seven causes and
// includes it), unlike the runtime-errored arm, which is reported and deliberately NOT gating. So
// the fix moved rows from a non-gating arm into a gating one, and this removal is the remedy the
// floor's own message names.
//
// THE POPULATION IS 82, NOT THE SEVENTEEN AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS BLOCK NAMED, and the
// correction is recorded here rather than silently swapped because the two numbers are two
// measurements of one fact and the stale one was load-bearing. Seventeen was measured before this
// branch carried main; the roster it was measured against no longer exists. Run 32670979986
// reports `stale_quarantine=82`, and all 82 are removed here.
//
// THE FOUR generated_coproduct_exhaustiveness_* ROWS ARE PART OF THAT 82 AND ARE REMOVED WITH IT.
// An earlier revision of this block retained them and called the pair the discriminating half of
// the edit, on the ground that they sat in the same generated module and were NOT reported
// passing. That was true of the measurement it was written against and is false of this one: the
// same run names all four explicitly as STALE-QUARANTINE. Retaining them on the strength of the
