// chunk_23 CARRIES ONE ROW AND IT ENTERS THIS ROSTER BY A ROUTE THE HEADER DOES NOT DESCRIBE, so
// the route is written here rather than left for the next reader to infer from the identity.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT IS CARRIED HERE RATHER THAN CITED, deliberately: a row that has told an
// assertion not to assert is exactly where a reader most needs the reason, and a pointer to a
// pull request is worthless to them if that request has not landed (or never does). The four
// facts, standalone:
//
//   1. WHAT THE WITNESS ASSERTS. `FreeMonoid<Node>` passed to a parameter declared
//      `FreeMonoid<NormalizedTree>` -- a container-element type mismatch at a direct call
//      argument -- must be REFUSED. The witness holds `!compile_dag_rust_emit_check(...)`, so it
//      passes only when the embedded fixture fails to compile.
//   2. IT IS ACCEPTED, AND THE CONTROLS ARE WHAT ESTABLISH IT. Two fixtures carrying the IDENTICAL
//      container-element mismatch, built from a locally declared record with no `Node`, no
//      `v2.std.node` and no occurrence identity anywhere in them -- one reusing the wrapper name
//      `NormalizedTree`, one using a corpus-unique wrapper name -- BOTH COMPILE CLEAN ON MAIN
//      (@ ddb7f533e4e), zero blocking diagnostics. They share nothing with the occurrence-identity
//      cut, so the acceptance is a property of the compiler and not of this branch.
//   3. THE PREVIOUS GREEN WAS NOT ITS OWN ASSERTION. On main the real fixture refuses with exactly
//      `UnresolvedType std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId` x2 and `ScopedOccurrenceRef` x1, and
//      carries NO type-mismatch diagnostic at all. `v2.std.node` referenced those two types fully
//      qualified WITHOUT importing that module, so every closure importing `v2.std.node` inherited
//      them. This branch adds the import, they resolve, and the accidental refusal goes with them.
//   4. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER. `container_element_nominal_brand_mismatch` reaching the
//      direct-call-argument position.
//
// Measured with the witness's own oracle (`compile_dag_rust_emit_check`) alongside
// `compile_dag_diagnostic_census`, over both corpora, 2026-08-27.
//
// Every other row here names a witness that FAILS and that someone is repairing. This one names a
// witness whose SUBJECT was never guarded, and the repair it waits on is not a repair to the
// witness -- it is a compiler wall that does not exist yet. The header's rule (a row belongs here
// only while someone is fixing it) is stated so this roster cannot become a skip list, and this
// row is admitted against the letter of that rule with the tension named rather than hidden: it
// has a trigger, it is at identity grain, and it is on the same self-emptying path as everything
// else -- the moment the wall lands, the witness passes, this roster reds the build naming this
// row, and the row is deleted. What it does NOT have is an owner mid-repair today.
//
// THE CLASS IS RUNG INFLATION, DESIGN 4b(1) -- NOT a 4b(3) rung drop, and the distinction decides
// what a reader may do with this row. Nothing regressed. The container-element mismatch judgment
// at a direct call argument does not fire and never did:
// `record_literal_container_element_mismatch_at_call_arg_still_refuses` was GREEN ON MAIN because
// `v2.std.node` referenced `std.occurrence_identity.OccurrenceId` and `ScopedOccurrenceRef` fully
// qualified WITHOUT importing that module, so every closure importing `v2.std.node` inherited two
// `UnresolvedType` diagnostics and refused for THAT reason. Measured on main @ ddb7f533e4e with the
// witness's own oracle, the fixture's refusal is exactly those two diagnostics and carries no
// type-mismatch diagnostic at all; the same container-element mismatch built from a locally
// declared record, with no `Node` and no `v2.std.node` in it, COMPILES CLEAN ON MAIN. This branch
// adds the import that resolves them, and the accidental refusal goes with them.
//
// SO THE ROW A READER MUST NOT WRITE is "restore the previous behaviour". The previous behaviour
// was an accident, and re-adding unresolved references to buy back the red would be manufacturing
// a refusal from a defect -- which is what this roster exists to make visible, not to launder.
//
// A witness green on a defect in its own closure is NEGATIVE coverage, not weak coverage: it is
// cited as evidence the wall is live, so the class never ranks for climbing. The nominal sibling
// `record_literal_nominal_mismatch_at_call_arg_still_refuses` stays green through a DIFFERENT path
// (`literal_introduction_type_mismatch`, whose actual is a record literal rather than a call),
// which is why the family read as coherent and was not.
//
// The class row and its ceiling are ALSO filed in
// `docs/plans/compiler-guarantee-recovery-gap-analysis.md` (gunbc#9457, against main, because the
// finding is about main) -- in ADDITION to the four facts above, never instead of them, because
// merge order is not this branch's to control and that row may land after this one or not at all.
// THE ENROLMENT COULD NOT LAND THERE and the reason is mechanical: on main
// this witness still PASSES, `required_floor_outcome_is_clean` requires `stale_quarantine` to be
// empty, and an enrolled row that passes reds the build -- so enrolling it on main would red
// main's own required floor. It fails here, which is the only tree where it is honestly enrollable.
//
// REMOVAL TRIGGER: `container_element_nominal_brand_mismatch` reaching the direct-call-argument
// position -- i.e. `nominal_call_arg_brand_mismatch` admitting two distinct nominal element types
// under a shared container template. The witness is the ready-made RED for that work and needs no
// new fixture.
fn floor_expected_red_chunk_23() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "test.claim.record_literal_call_arg_handoff_witness.record_literal_container_element_mismatch_at_call_arg_still_refuses", tail: Empty {} }
}

// THIRTEEN FURTHER IDENTITIES RETIRED 2026-08-26 BY ONE REPAIR, and the number is the finding
// rather than the bookkeeping. The local-binding-shadow repair -- a call site spelling a name held
// by both a local binding and a module-level free function now reaches the LOCAL -- was enrolled
// with ONE row of its own. On the first required run after it landed, thirteen OTHER enrolled reds
// across five unrelated modules passed: test.claim.host_phase_status (2),
// test.claim.interpreter_replacement_cut_witness_test (2), test.claim.srv3_subsumption (6),
// v2.test.claim.staging (2) and v2.test.emit.produced_decl_support_preserved (1). None of them was
// authored as a shadow probe and none names the class; they were filed as ordinary reds against
// their own subjects, which is exactly what a SILENT wrong value looks like from the consumer's
// side -- the fold returns a plausible answer, the assertion disagrees with it, and the row gets
// written against the assertion. So this list was carrying thirteen symptoms of one cause, and the
// cause was legible from none of them.
//
// THE ROWS ARE REMOVED AND THE WITNESSES ARE NOT (DESIGN 4b(4)). Every one of those claims stays
// enrolled and now passes, which is what keeps the repair's reach measured rather than asserted:
// if the gate regresses, thirteen independent modules go red before the shadow fixture does.

// THE LIVE-TREE-ADMISSION CHUNK -- THE OTHER HALF OF gunbc#9106, a completion rather than a new
// admission.
//
// IT IS NOT NUMBERED, AND THE REASON IS A DEFECT THAT WAS ABOUT TO LAND RATHER THAN A STYLE
// PREFERENCE. Three open branches each mint a `floor_expected_red_chunk_24` into this file:
// gunbc#9591 (this one), gunbc#9587 (one add-slice row) and gunbc#9569 (six
// `sole_constructor_completeness_audit_probe_test` rows, which are also six of the 47 below). The
// numeric suffix is a SHARED MUTABLE COUNTER whose next value every concurrent lane computes
// independently from the same base, so collision is not a risk under concurrency -- it is the
// expected outcome.
//
// WHAT MAKES IT WORSE THAN AN ORDINARY CONFLICT, and why a distinct name is the construction
// rather than the courtesy: two lanes appending a same-named `fn` at DIFFERENT offsets can merge
// with NO CONFLICT MARKERS, leaving one file carrying two definitions of one name. This
// repository has measured what happens next -- `test.claim.duplicate_definition_binding_probe_test`
// exists because a duplicate definition is SILENTLY ACCEPTED and the later binding wins, so the
// merge would not fail, it would quietly drop one lane's rows and stay green. A position-derived
// name is a second naming scheme for something the declaration already names (DESIGN section 3),
// and this is that rule's cost arriving in the merge graph.
//
// So the chunk is named for WHAT IT ADMITS. A meaning-carrying name cannot be independently
// derived by two lanes, which makes the collision unrepresentable instead of detected.
//
// #9106 deleted the floor's stale live-tree decline. That decline was a FILE-GRAIN PREDICTION
// that a witness reading the live tree could not participate in the hermetic fold, and it had
// stopped agreeing with what the interpreter does. Deleting it was correct and it admitted a
// population that had never executed: on main run 33145062452 (3a8344b5c3) the floor reports
// failed=47, and these are those 47.
//
// WHY THEY BELONG HERE AND NOT SOMEWHERE ELSE, which is the question this roster's header says
// to ask before enrolling anything. Every one of them EXECUTES AND ANSWERS FALSE -- the run
// classifies each as `FAIL ... returned Bool(false)`, which is the semantic verdict this roster
// is defined over. None of them route-gaps (no overlap with `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap`), none
// produces a non-verdict, and none is a budget outcome.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THAT SENTENCE ALSO CLAIMED EACH ROW "REACHES ITS SUBJECT", AND THE RUN
// DOES NOT ESTABLISH THAT. `Bool` has no spelling for "I could not observe my subject", so an
// unreached subject and a genuine NO render identically as `returned Bool(false)`. Execution is
// established; subject-reachability is not, and the difference is exactly the class this
// document names as execution-provenance loss. The clause is removed rather than softened,
// because a reader quoting it would have been quoting a property nothing measured. The live
// candidate is named in the follow-ups below.
// That last exclusion is load-bearing and it is the reason this chunk closes two of the run's
// four causes rather than all four. The same run reports 44 `interrupted_before_verdict` and 2
// `completed_over_cost_requirement`, and NONE of them is here.
//
// WHY NOT, stated as a refusal rather than as a scope note: an interrupted row produced NO
// VERDICT. Enrolling it would assert "this witness runs and fails, and someone is fixing it"
// about an identity that never answered -- which is precisely the 101-row mistake this file's
// own header opens with, and `ExpectedRedArm` refuses budget outcomes by construction so the
// enrolment would not even take. The 2 completed-over-cost rows answered, but what they owe is
// a COST and not a failure; enrolling them would assert an expected FAILURE they do not
// exhibit. Cost is not a verdict, and the verdict roster cannot carry it.
//
// THE POPULATION IS BOUNDED AND MEASURED, at identity grain, on the run named above: 44
// interrupted, all on the CPU clock against `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`'s
// 5000ms, concentrated in live-tree corpus witnesses (13 in
// `v2.test.claim.enforcement.grammar_coverage_witness`, 6 in
// `v2.test.claim.enforcement_live_witness`, 6 in
// `v2.test.claim.complexity.accumulator_copy_roster_gate`, the rest spread across 10 modules);
// and 2 completed-over-cost, both in `test.claim.transport_script_wall_compile_red`, on the
// wall clock at 18882ms and 19024ms against 10000ms. Every interrupted figure is a LOWER BOUND
// -- the deadline preempted the measurement -- so their real cost is unmeasured and the sum is
// not this population's cost.
//
// WHERE THEY GO, AND IT IS NOT A NEW MECHANISM. `v2.workflow.required_floor` states the
// remedies exhaustively -- reduce what the witness reaches for, or enrol it in a lane declaring
// its own dated ceiling -- and names relocation as forbidden. A carrier for exactly this axis is
// built and open as gunbc#9517 (`v2.workflow.floor_cost_debt`, joined by a `DeclinedCostDebt`
// arm in `required_floor`). These 46 identities were handed to that lane at identity grain with
// clock, figure and limit per row, rather than duplicated here: authoring them into a module
// that is not on main would fork the authority this chunk exists to respect.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS PARAGRAPH ASSERTED THAT #9517's ROSTER RETURNS `Empty`, AND SAID
// SO AS A FIRST-HAND READING. The reading was honest -- `floor_cost_debt_chunks()` on
// `origin/session/witty-wren-148` was `Empty {}` when it was taken -- and it was WRONG WITHIN
// THE HOUR, because the branch was moving while I read it. That is this document's own
// transcribed-measurement class arriving on the shortest possible clock: a bare present-tense
// claim about ANOTHER LANE'S HEAD is not merely stale-prone, it has no producer on this side of
// the boundary that could ever re-derive it, so nothing here refuses when it goes false. The
// sentence is deleted rather than re-pinned to a newer number, because a second number would
// rot the same way.
//
// WHAT IS KNOWN AND WHAT IS NOT, kept separate: that lane reports the 46 enrolled as a
// separately-labelled cost population, explicitly NOT claimed to pass, keyed on MARGINAL cpu so
// that draining a row displaces no shared fill onto its successor. That is their measurement,
// reported to this lane and not re-derived by it. What THIS module can stand behind is only its
// own side: these 46 are absent from this roster, deliberately, and the reason is stated above.
//
// RUNG, DECLARED HONESTLY (DESIGN 4b(3)): on MAIN the cost axis stays BELOW the floor's own bar
// -- the run still stops on it, so nothing is silently admitted, but 46 identities reach no
// usable verdict every run and no mechanism ON MAIN holds them. Bounded, identity-grain,
// re-measurable. RESTORATION TRIGGER: a roster ON MAIN carries these 46 under an O=R admission,
// at which point they are counted debt on the cost axis instead of an ungoverned cause. The
// trigger names the CAPABILITY and deliberately not an artifact (DESIGN 4b(3), 2026-08-26): NOT
// "#9517 merges", because a merge of an empty roster closes none of them, and NOT "that lane
// enrols them", because an enrolment on an unmerged branch changes nothing about what the
// required run on main observes.
//
// THE TREE ALREADY DEMANDED THIS, AND THE RUN CONFIRMED IT BY EXECUTION.
//
// The prediction was registered before the run: enrolling
// `legacy_test_behavior_unclassified_frontier_is_zero` would satisfy the assertion the
// `quarantine_probe_disposition_witness_test` claims make ABOUT this roster, so they would stop
// failing and report STALE-QUARANTINE. Run 33154432928 answered `failed=0 stale_quarantine=3`,
// naming exactly those three, and they are removed here -- by the roster's own removal path,
// which is the mechanism working rather than an edit correcting a mistake.
//
// THE PREDICTION SAID FOUR AND THE ANSWER WAS THREE, and the error is recorded because it is
// the more instructive half. The fourth name was
// `legacy_test_behavior_unclassified_frontier_is_zero` itself, and it did NOT flip -- correctly.
// It is the row the join is ABOUT, not a row that passes as a consequence: it still fails on its
// own subject, and this roster still holds it. Conflating "the identity a witness names" with
// "an identity that changes state when the witness is satisfied" is what produced the extra row,
// and a join has both roles in it at once.
//
// WHY THE ENROLMENT WAS STILL RIGHT FOR ALL THREE. They failed on main and they answer false, so
// they met this roster's admission when they were added; what removed them is that the same
// change repaired their subject. A roster that could not hold a row for one run and release it
// on the next would force the author to predict the repair perfectly before landing it.
// `test.claim.quarantine_probe_disposition_witness_test.the_former_live_tree_declined_row_is_now_expected_red`
// asserts that `test.claim.legacy_test_behavior_disposition_acceptance_test.legacy_test_behavior_unclassified_frontier_is_zero`
// is held by this roster -- it was authored against the post-#9106 world and has been failing
// since, because the row it names was never added. That witness is itself one of the 47. So
// four of these rows (the three `quarantine_probe_disposition_witness_test` claims and the
// `legacy_test_behavior` one they join against) are expected to leave this chunk on the first
// run after it lands, by this roster's own removal path rather than by an edit.
//
// THE 47 ARE NOT ONE CAUSE, AND THREE GROUPS INSIDE THEM CARRY A REMEDY THIS ROSTER CANNOT
// STATE. A row here is a bare identity string with no reason field, so enrolment says exactly
// one thing -- this is expected to fail -- about rows that fail for at least six distinct
// causes with different remedies. That is a real limit of the mechanism, not of this chunk, and
// the three groups are named here so the next reader does not conclude one cause. All three are
// FOLLOW-UPS rather than reasons to withhold enrolment, because enrolment is a REVERSIBLE
// HOLDING STATE WITH A LOUD EXIT: the floor refuses on an enrolled row that starts passing and
// names it, which is the same path by which this very change removes one.
//
//   1. THE THREE `guarantee_floor_class_probe_witness` GENERIC-INSTANTIATION ROWS FAIL BECAUSE
//      A WALL LANDED, NOT BECAUSE THE HOLE IS OPEN. They assert a hole is still open; it is
//      not. The discriminating evidence is that BOTH of that hole's controls PASS
//      (`floor_generic_instantiation_monomorphic_control_refuses`,
//      `floor_generic_instantiation_green_control_is_clean`) and the SIBLING hole probe
//      `floor_field_through_generics_is_still_silently_accepted` also passes -- so the harness
//      reached the judgment and the other hole is genuinely still open. A harness that saw
//      nothing would have taken the sibling down too. That module's own
//      `guarantee_floor_class_probe_scope_note` prescribes the remedy verbatim: such a probe
//      "flip[s] RED on the day the wall lands, which is the signal to rewrite the row as
//      `ExpectBlockingRefusal` rather than to delete the probe" -- which is DESIGN 4b(4)'s rule
//      that a greened expecting-red probe becomes a permanent regression control. Enrolment
//      holds them honestly (they do fail) while recording nothing about WHY, so the rewrite is
//      owed and the roster's stale-quarantine arm is what will demand it.
//
//   2. THE FOUR `sole_constructor_completeness_audit_probe_test` f10 ROWS ANSWERED AN OPEN QUESTION
//      THE FIRST TIME THEY RAN. Their block annotation states a question rather than a marked
//      red -- "to test whether resolution of an unqualified bare-name construction depends on
//      import statement order" -- and the answer is YES: `_ab` fails while `_ba` passes on
//      identical source with the imports swapped, and the two direct probes fail in OPPOSITE
//      directions, both predicted by last-import-wins. That is the census-AMBIGUOUS resolution
//      hole DESIGN 4b already records as decidable once scoped. The distinction is decidable in
//      the file rather than a matter of judgement: f13 and f19, which ARE enrolled here on the
//      same footing, carry an explicit "Deliberately RED" marking and the f10 four do not.
//
//   3. THE THREE `cost_coverage_witness` ROWS ARE THE SUBJECT-REACHABILITY CANDIDATE. That
//      module has 10 test fns; the 7 that pass are the ones that survive an empty subject (one
//      reads `(receipt.files_attempted == 0) || ...`) and the 3 that fail are the ones demanding
//      non-zero content. That is consistent with a genuine NO and equally consistent with a
//      subject that was never reached, and no run distinguishes them. Enrolled as failing, which
//      is what was observed; NOT asserted to be semantic.
//
// The classification, controls and per-row execution behind all three are crisp-newt-899's
// triage, verified here against the cited files rather than accepted on report.
//
// ONE STALE PREMISE FOUND WHILE CHECKING THE ABOVE, recorded because it is load-bearing for a
// carrier this chunk overlaps. `gunbc.declined_live_tree_defect_classification` states that it
// "is not an expected-red enrolment and must never become one: enrolling a row here would assert
// that the floor runs it and that it fails, and the floor does not run it at all." Eight of the
// modules it classifies contain rows enrolled here, and the floor DOES now run them -- they are
// in run 33145062452's FAIL lines. So that clause's REASON has been overtaken by #9106; the
// clause is not wrong about authority substitution in general, it is premised on a
// non-execution that no longer holds. Not edited here, because it is that carrier's to correct
// and a second account of one fact is the defect either way -- reported to its owner instead.
//
// WHAT THIS CHUNK IS NOT. It is not a coverage claim and it is not a disposition. Each row is a
// defect somebody owns, visible at identity grain, and the chunk can only shrink under an
// honest run -- a member that starts passing reds the build naming itself. Its length is the
// debt #9106 surfaced, and the debt was always there; what changed is that it is now counted.
// ONE MEMBER OF THIS CHUNK HAS A PER-ROW MECHANISM, MEASURED BY EXECUTION, and it is written
// here because the bulk enrolment above establishes THAT the row fails and not WHY -- and for
// this row the why is what stops the next reader closing it the cheap way.
//
// `v2.test.execution.self_host_candidate_generation.candidate_generation_translate_self_emit_dag_add_slice_holds`
// runs `generate_translate_self_emit_candidate` -- `infer` composed with `translate` -- over the
// dag add slice and holds only on `Accepted`, with the emitted node equal to
// `dag_emitted_add_fn_node()` and no diagnostic carried.
//
// A RECEIPT, AND EXPLICITLY NOT AN INSTRUMENT -- the distinction is stated because DESIGN's
// name-the-instrument-never-transcribe-its-output ruling reaches this paragraph, and half of it
// bites here. At main 3a8344b5c, a probe module returning each stage's verdict as a Symbol
// answered `infer_accepted` for `infer(dag_add_emitted_root)` and `infer_grounding_not_derived`
// for `generate_translate_self_emit_candidate` over that same root and `dag_add_target_model`.
// Infer ACCEPTS; the composition refuses.
//
// WHAT THE RULING DOES AND DOES NOT REACH HERE. Its subject is a live claim transcribed as a
// standing figure, which rots because the thing it describes moves. This one names a fixed tree,
// so it stays true of that tree forever -- the dated-receipt half the ruling preserves. What it
// DOES reach is reproducibility: the probe was a throwaway module and no `.dag` entry point
// re-derives this, so a reader cannot re-run it. That gap is recorded here rather than papered
// over, and it is this note's own next-rung trigger: a `.dag` entry returning the per-stage
// verdicts for one root would let this paragraph name a producer instead of a commit.
//
// IT IS NOT DELETED IN FAVOUR OF THE STRUCTURAL ACCOUNT BELOW, because that account cannot
// establish it. Reading the gate tells you translate CAN refuse an underived grounding; it
// cannot tell you that infer ACCEPTED this input rather than rejecting it earlier, and that is
// the fact locating the work in the derivation rules rather than in translate. A structural
// argument that reached the same conclusion without the observation would be the inference this
// repository keeps recording as a diagnostic name read as a mechanism.
//
// THE REFUSAL IS THE DECLARED FRONTIER, NOT A DEFECT IN EITHER STAGE. The fixture root is
// `TypeNode { connective: Arrow }` over `Conj` inhabitants, and `v2.compiler.infer`
// `node_grounding_frontier_note` states that v2 derives grounding for four of the closed
// vocabulary's twelve kinds (Branch, Match, Loop, and Transform in its add shape) and no other.
// So infer records `GroundingNotDerived`, and `translate_grounding_derived_gate_subtree` routes
// that variant through `canonical_grounding_from_inferred_facts` and refuses with the frontier's
// own typed reason. The gate is doing what it was built to do on a tree the frontier has not
// reached.
//
// AND NO REPAIR TO THE GATE ALONE WOULD GREEN THIS ROW, so a reader must not read the refusal as
// locating the work in translate: `inferred_facts_not_derived` returns the frontier diagnostic on
// infer's ACCEPTED path, so even an accepting translate leaves the witness's `d == None` conjunct
// false. Both conjuncts fail for the one cause.
//
// WHY THE CHEAP GREEN IS FORBIDDEN, which is the whole reason this note exists. The sibling
// witnesses in `v2.test.execution.dag_add_emit_round_trip` pass on the SAME fixture because they
// build their `InferredTree` from a hand-authored facts lookup answering `DerivedGrounding`.
// Making `infer` answer that way for connective nodes generally is precisely the `root == root`
// grounding `node_grounding_frontier_note` records as the defect the frontier carrier REPLACED --
// every node typed as itself, read downstream as an established judgment. The honest repair is a
// derivation rule for the kinds this slice uses, which is v2 self-host lane work and not a
// witness edit.
//
// ITS REMOVAL TRIGGER IS NOT RESTATED HERE AND DELIBERATELY SO: it is already modeled, with its
// population and blocker, in `gunbc.guarantee_rung_drop`
// `self_host_candidate_generation_add_slice_stall`. Copying those fields into a comment would
// fork that authority into prose that drifts on its own (DESIGN 2/3, and 4c's rule that an
// annotation must not restate what a declaration says), and no `Accepted` program can read a
// comment, so the copy could never be the thing that is checked. What this roster owns is the
// RELATION: this row is that stall's executing consumer, so when the stall's trigger is met the
// witness passes, stale quarantine reds the build naming the row, and the two delete together.
fn floor_expected_red_chunk_live_tree_admission() -> List<String> {
